---
title: Dev inbox
name: Dev inbox
description: A local mailbox that catches every email your app sends in development, so you can read the real HTML instead of a console dump — no config, no second tool.
category: Guides
---

Mail you send while building has to go somewhere. Sending it for real means a stray
customer address is one typo away; printing it to the terminal means you never see the
thing you're actually building.

So Postboi ships a local inbox. Start your dev server and mail lands in a mailbox at
`/__postboi` instead of going out — rendered HTML, headers, attachments and all.

**Your code doesn't change.** No `preview()`, no `if (dev)`, no separate provider. The same
`mail()` call that sends in production is captured in development.

## Vite projects — nothing to run

If you have the [`postboi/vite`](/cloudflare-workers#the-config-file) plugin in your
`vite.config.ts` (`postboi init` adds it), the inbox is already there:

```bash
bun run dev
```

```
  ➜  Local:    http://localhost:5173/
  ➜  Postboi:  dev inbox at http://localhost:5173/__postboi
```

It rides on the dev server you already started — no extra port, no second terminal. That
covers SvelteKit, Astro, Nuxt, Remix and plain Vite.

Because it's mounted by Vite's `configureServer` hook, which only ever runs in dev, the
inbox cannot end up in a production build.

## Everything else — `postboi dev`

Express, Hono, Next.js and bare `wrangler dev` have no Vite to hang it off, so run the
inbox yourself:

```bash
bunx postboi dev
```

```
  Postboi dev inbox: http://localhost:1080/__postboi
  Mail from this project is captured here instead of being sent.
```

It advertises its port in `node_modules/.postboi/`, so an app started in the same project
directory finds it with nothing configured. Running somewhere else — another directory, a
container, a different runtime — set `POSTBOI_INBOX` to the port:

```bash
POSTBOI_INBOX=1080 bun run dev
```

## Just looking at it

`--demo` fills the inbox with sample mail — a styled HTML message, a FormData table with
an attachment, and a text-only body — so there's something to look at without wiring an app
to it:

```bash
bunx postboi dev --demo
```

It re-seeds on every start, which also makes it the way to work on the inbox itself: run it
under a file watcher and an edit restarts the server with the sample mail still there.

```bash
bun --watch src/cli/index.ts dev --demo
```

## What gets captured

**Everything**, including a provider with real, working credentials. That's the point: a
laptop should not be able to mail a real person because a test fixture had a real address
in it. With the inbox running, `mail()` never reaches a provider.

Two guards keep that from ever biting a deploy:

- It only happens when `NODE_ENV` is exactly `development`. An unrecognised environment is
  treated as production, so nothing is ever silently swallowed on a server.
- If the inbox isn't reachable — you killed the dev server, the port moved — the message is
  **printed to the console**, never sent. A send you thought was captured can't quietly
  become real mail.

## Turning it off

To send for real from your machine, pick whichever fits:

```ts
// postboi.config.ts
import { config } from 'postboi'

export default config({
	dev: { inbox: false },
})
```

```bash
POSTBOI_INBOX=off bun run dev
```

```ts
// vite.config.ts — don't serve it at all
postboi({ inbox: false })
```

## Reading the mail

The inbox lists messages newest-first and shows each one four ways:

| Tab             | What it shows                                                       |
| --------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| **Message**     | The HTML, rendered in a sandboxed frame — scripts blocked, as a real client would |
| **Plain Text**  | The `text` part, including the one [auto-derived](/config) from your HTML |
| **Source**      | The raw HTML, for when a provider mangles something                 |
| **Attachments** | Every file, downloadable, with its type and size                    |

Above them: `from`, `to`, `cc`, `bcc` and `reply_to` exactly as the provider would have
received them — after defaults are applied and addresses parsed. If `reply_to` isn't what
you expected, this is where you find out.

Messages live in memory for the life of the dev server. Restarting it empties the inbox.

### The windows are real windows

The mailbox and the reader are proper MDI children: **drag** them by the title bar,
**resize** from the right edge, bottom edge or the corner grip, **maximise** with the box
in the title bar or a double-click, and **minimise** to the taskbar. Clicking a taskbar
button restores a window — or minimises it if it's already focused, as the real one did.

That matters more than the nostalgia: the reading pane is only ever big enough because you
can make it bigger. **Prev** and **Next** at the bottom of the reader step through the
mailbox without going back to the list.

### The CRT

The whole thing renders inside a cathode-ray tube. The screen's edges genuinely bow —
that's an SVG clip path, so it scales with the window — and over it sit a phosphor mask
(coloured RGB gradients blended with `overlay`, which modulates hue the way phosphor does,
rather than black lines that just look like dirt), film grain, corner falloff, a glass
highlight, flicker, a slow roll, and glow spilling onto the bezel.

It's CSS over the normal DOM rather than a shader, because the message preview is an iframe
and an iframe can't go through one. That's also why the *content* stays flat while the edges
curve: bending live DOM needs a displacement map, and it would bend the mail you're reading.

Scanlines across an email you're checking the design of are the opposite of useful, so the
**CRT** button in the toolbar turns all of it off, and the choice is remembered. Flicker and
the rolling band also respect `prefers-reduced-motion`; the static texture stays, since it's
the movement that makes people queasy.

### Sound

New mail announces itself, and there's a greeting on load and a sign-off on Shut Down — the
real 1990s ones. The **Sound** button in the toolbar mutes it, and the choice is remembered,
because a dev tool that says "Welcome!" in an open-plan office is a dev tool people
uninstall. Browsers won't play anything until you've clicked the page once, so the first
greeting usually waits for you.

### Signing on

Opening the inbox dials in first — _Locating mailroom… Connecting to localhost…
Intercepting outgoing mail…_ — then greets you. It's about two and a half seconds, **Cancel**
skips it, and it's pure theatre: the inbox is already loaded and listening behind the
dialog, so cancelling costs nothing but the joke.

### Configuring the defaults

The CRT, the sound and the sign-on can each start off, for everyone on the project:

```ts
// vite.config.ts
postboi({ inbox: { crt: false, sounds: false, intro: false } })
```

```bash
bunx postboi dev --no-crt --no-sound --no-intro
```

These set what the page *starts* with. The toolbar toggles still work, and a viewer's own
choice is remembered and wins — so muting the project doesn't stop anyone turning it back on
for themselves. `inbox: false` disables the whole thing.

### Yes, there's a Start menu

Press the **Windows key** (**Cmd** on macOS) and Windows 98's Start menu opens, with Check
Mail Now, the CRT toggle, help, and a Shut Down that does exactly what you'd hope. Only a
bare press counts — held as a modifier it's left alone, since that's how you copy and
reload.

## Tests still use the mock

The inbox is for reading mail with your eyes. In tests, keep using
[`postboi/mock`](/providers#mock-provider), which captures to `sent` with no server
involved:

```ts
import Mock from 'postboi/mock'

const mail = new Mock({ default: { from: 'no-reply@example.com' } })
await mail.send({ to: 'contact@example.com', subject: 'Hi', body: '<p>Hello</p>' })

expect(mail.sent).toHaveLength(1)
```

> The inbox stands in front of **sending** only. `mail.lists`, `mail.contacts` and the other
> [Postboi provider](/provider) namespaces still talk to the real API in development —
> managing an audience isn't something you'd want faked.
